{"title":"1999 OIM Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_OIM_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given two circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, we say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> bisects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> if the common chord is a diameter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Consider two fixed non-concentric circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/b/70b97eb4ad6e4e7a5a97e2547d9ed6e965960d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/5/b65e6facfe122c182fe14d503aeace2c52a9739d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>a) Prove that there are infinitely many circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> bisects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/b/70b97eb4ad6e4e7a5a97e2547d9ed6e965960d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> bisects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/5/b65e6facfe122c182fe14d503aeace2c52a9739d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>b) Find the locus of the centers of circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>~translated into English by Tomas Diaz. ~orders@tomasdiaz.com\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram\">Diagram</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_OIM_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>One such example\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/9/a79c48f7dfb5bbc4070dc71bca8bb76e4bec808c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(200);  pair B=(0,0), C1=(-3,0), C2=(3,0); real r=2; real R=3.605551275; // sqrt(13)  draw(circle(B,R)); draw(circle(C1,r),blue); draw(circle(C2,r),blue);  draw((-3,-2)--(-3,2)); draw((3,-2)--(3,2));  dot(B); dot(C1); dot(C2);  label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,S); label(&quot;$C_1$&quot;,C1,S); label(&quot;$C_2$&quot;,C2,S);  pair O1=(-3,2), O2=(-3,-2), O3=(3,2), O4=(3,-2);  dot(O1); dot(O2); dot(O3); dot(O4);  label(&quot;$O_1$&quot;,O1,NW); label(&quot;$O_2$&quot;,O2,SW); label(&quot;$O_3$&quot;,O3,NE); label(&quot;$O_4$&quot;,O4,SE); [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"242\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_OIM_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>a) To prove that there are infinite of such circles, we must first prove there always exists such a circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> satisfying the conditions if and only if the two circles are not concentric. \n<b>Lemma 1</b> There always exists a circle that bisects circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/8/418d43d60810a2d318ec566fe2fdedaf702c56fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_1, C_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"47\" height=\"16\" />. <b>Proof</b> To prove this, first draw diameters <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/7/127e372b5c305f6505bc9497ec419dbe77565fbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1O_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/8/f78b3ee5dd71a306cef5f7e6c5aaf8f219cb3ef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_3O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"17\" />, representing the intersection points of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> with the other circles. (as shown in the example above). Secondly, draw lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/b/46bb3c043b2592bd062cc72fd446df30ffdcead1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1O_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/c/72c03ceae00c4a484a798ce1c8c9db63b2a8eab7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_2O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"17\" />. Any such circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> which satisfies the conditions provided must inscribe quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b9f9eadf3e73a92d5292ba203920e07f42878df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1O_2O_3O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"17\" />. This is because all points must lie on any such circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> (as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/7/127e372b5c305f6505bc9497ec419dbe77565fbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1O_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/8/f78b3ee5dd71a306cef5f7e6c5aaf8f219cb3ef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_3O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"17\" /> are chords of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />). For any such quadrilateral to be inscribable, opposite angles must be supplementary. For quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b9f9eadf3e73a92d5292ba203920e07f42878df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1O_2O_3O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"17\" />, this is always true, as by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/5/35557d595cea6872371d6061d163bde47e29d3f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_2O_3=O_1O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"17\" />, the figure is always either a rectangle or a isosceles trapezoid, both of which by definition have opposite sides adding up to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/b/5cb1b41850ab676e4d1cf15c69b1609af4befbc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> degrees. This means that quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b9f9eadf3e73a92d5292ba203920e07f42878df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1O_2O_3O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"17\" /> can always be inscribed within a circle, thus there always exists circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> which bisects both circles. <b>Conclusion</b> For the second part of the proof, we must acknowledge that not all combinations of diameters of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/8/998275cf9e701a5b3c6e3ae7692aa195c6a6f666.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_1,C_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"47\" height=\"16\" /> make a cyclic quadrilateral. However, as there are infinite diameters (as they are just a segment created by 2 points on a circle which are both mutually colinear to the center), there are infinite combinations of diameters which make a cyclic quadrilateral out of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b9f9eadf3e73a92d5292ba203920e07f42878df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_1O_2O_3O_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"17\" />. One can see this, for example, in the converse of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ptolemy%27s_Theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Ptolemy&#39;s Theorem\">Ptolemy's Theorem</a>, which states that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/5/8d54c8a88b48323598d8d61a01f92bb4c4b948d0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_1O_3 \\cdot O_2O_4 + O_1O_2 \\cdot O_3O_4 = O_2O_3 \\cdot O_1O_4\\]\" width=\"340\" height=\"15\" /> We can see, intuitively, that they are infinite number of 4 such points which satisfy the conditions. Thus, there are infinite such circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. (Note that this proof may be a little weak at the end)\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_OIM_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.oma.org.ar/enunciados/ibe14.htm\">https://www.oma.org.ar/enunciados/ibe14.htm</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017193032\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.017 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.039 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 192/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6835/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:26122:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017193032 and revision id 286307. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}